/*! @brief The abstract interface that provides the character-handling members shared by
 text, comment and CDATA nodes

 XmlCharacterData is the superinterface of XmlText and XmlComment nodes. Documents never
 contain XmlCharacterData nodes; they contain only XmlText, XmlComment and
 XmlCDATASection nodes. The interface is abstract: fibjs does not export it (typeof
 XmlCharacterData is undefined) and it cannot be constructed, so the members are
 documented here once and used through the concrete classes.

 The node family (see the XmlNode class comment for the tree members):
 - XmlText (nodeType 3, nodeName `#text`) — plain text in an element or attribute;
 - XmlComment (8, `#comment`) — a comment;
 - XmlCDATASection (4, `#cdata-section`) — unparsed character data; it extends XmlText,
   so it inherits both this interface and splitText.

 Concepts:

 - **data, nodeValue and textContent**: data is the string stored in the node and is
   writable; nodeValue (declared by XmlNode) is an alias for the same storage on these
   nodes. textContent is an alias only on XmlText: on XmlComment and XmlCDATASection
   fibjs returns an empty string and ignores assignments (the standard returns the
   data), so read data or nodeValue instead.
 - **Offsets and lengths are UTF-16 code units**: length, offset and count are measured
   like JavaScript string indices, so a character outside the basic multilingual plane
   counts as two units and may be split by an offset that lands between its surrogate
   halves.
 - **Boundaries**: an offset greater than the current length throws a RangeError with
   number 20012 (the standard throws IndexSizeError); offset === length is valid and
   produces an empty string or a no-op. A count larger than the remaining text is
   clamped to the end, and a negative count is treated as "to the end" rather than
   rejected (the standard throws IndexSizeError). Only strings are accepted for data
   and arg; other types throw a type error (20005) instead of being converted.
 - **Not included**: the standard also lists ProcessingInstruction as an implementer of
   this interface, but in fibjs XmlProcessingInstruction is a plain XmlNode with its own
   target and data members, so it does not inherit the members below. The horizontal
   ChildNode helpers before, after, replaceWith and remove come from XmlNode instead.

 Obtained from (through the concrete classes):
 - text: `xml.parse('<a>t</a>').documentElement.firstChild`,
   `document.createTextNode(text)`, or `textNode.splitText(offset)`;
 - comments: `xml.parse('<r><!--c--></r>').documentElement.firstChild` or
   `document.createComment(data)`;
 - CDATA: `xml.parse('<r><![CDATA[d]]></r>').documentElement.firstChild` or
   `document.createCDATASection(data)`.

 Example 1 — the data members on a text node created with createTextNode:
 ```JavaScript
 const xml = require('xml');

 const doc = new xml.Document();
 const text = doc.createTextNode('hello');

 console.log(text.length);              // 5
 console.log(text.substringData(1, 3)); // ell
 text.appendData(' world');
 text.insertData(5, ',');
 console.log(text.data);                // hello, world
 text.replaceData(6, 6, 'there');
 console.log(text.data);                // hello,there
 text.deleteData(5, 1);
 console.log(text.data);                // hellothere
 ```

 Example 2 — the same members on a comment:
 ```JavaScript
 const xml = require('xml');

 const doc = xml.parse('<root><!--draft--><a/></root>');
 const comment = doc.documentElement.firstChild;

 console.log(comment.nodeName); // #comment
 console.log(comment.data);     // draft
 comment.replaceData(0, 5, 'final');
 console.log(String(doc.documentElement)); // <root><!--final--><a/></root>
 ```

 Example 3 — UTF-16 indexing and an out-of-range offset:
 ```JavaScript
 const xml = require('xml');

 const doc = new xml.Document();
 const text = doc.createTextNode('a\u{1F600}b');

 console.log(text.length);              // 4, the emoji counts as two code units
 console.log(text.substringData(1, 2)); // the emoji character

 try {
     text.insertData(9, 'x');
 } catch (err) {
     console.log(err.name);   // RangeError
     console.log(err.number); // 20012
 }
 ```
 */
interface XmlCharacterData : XmlNode
{
    /*! @brief Reads and writes the text contained in this node

     The setter replaces the whole character data and accepts only a string; any other
     value throws a type error (20005). On text, comment and CDATA nodes the same
     storage is exposed as nodeValue.

     Example — change the data of a text node:
     ```JavaScript
     const xml = require('xml');

     const doc = new xml.Document();
     const text = doc.createTextNode('draft');

     console.log(text.data);      // draft
     text.data = 'final';
     console.log(text.nodeValue); // final
     ```
    */
    String data;

    /*! @brief The number of characters contained in this node

     The count is in UTF-16 code units, matching the JavaScript string length, so a
     character outside the basic multilingual plane counts as two.
    */
    readonly Integer length;

    /*! @brief Extracts a substring from the node

     Count is clamped to the end of the data, and a negative count extracts everything
     from offset to the end. An offset greater than length throws a RangeError (20012);
     offset === length returns an empty string.

     Example — extract with clamping:
     ```JavaScript
     const xml = require('xml');

     const text = xml.parse('<r>abcdef</r>').documentElement.firstChild;

     console.log(text.substringData(2, 3));        // cde
     console.log(text.substringData(4, 99));       // ef
     console.log(text.substringData(6, 1).length); // 0
     ```

     @param offset the position of the first character to return
     @param count the number of characters in the substring to return
     @return returns the extracted string
    */
    String substringData(Integer offset, Integer count);

    /*! @brief Appends a string to the node

     The argument is appended verbatim (it is not parsed as markup); a non-string
     argument throws a type error (20005).

     @param arg the string to append to the node
    */
    appendData(String arg);

    /*! @brief Inserts a string into the node

     The characters are inserted before the character currently at offset; offset may
     equal length to append. An offset greater than length throws a RangeError (20012).

     @param offset the character position at which to insert the string into the node
     @param arg the string to insert
    */
    insertData(Integer offset, String arg);

    /*! @brief Deletes text from the node

     Count is clamped to the end of the data; a negative count is treated as "to the
     end". An offset greater than length throws a RangeError (20012), while deleting at
     offset === length is a no-op.

     @param offset the position of the first character to delete
     @param count the number of characters to delete
    */
    deleteData(Integer offset, Integer count);

    /*! @brief Replaces the characters of the node with the given string

     The count characters starting at offset are replaced by arg; count is clamped to
     the end of the data and a negative count replaces to the end. An offset greater
     than length throws a RangeError (20012).

     @param offset the character position in the node to replace
     @param count the number of characters to replace
     @param arg the string to insert
    */
    replaceData(Integer offset, Integer count, String arg);
};
